 {:id :EDITOR-SNAP-011
  :status :active
  :ears "When the visible editor snapshot is unavailable, a compound editor command shall refuse with a visible notification and shall send nothing."
  :misreadings
  ["A null snapshot is an empty snapshot — it is not: {sync: null} makes the
    server commit its OWN idea of the draft under a gesture the reader made
    against something they could not see."
   "The server will reject it anyway, so sending is harmless — /api/save with a
    nil sync skips snapshot validation entirely and proceeds to persist current
    state."
   "Silence is acceptable when nothing was sent — a Save gesture that produces
    no request and no message reads as a successful save to the reader."
   "Returning early may skip endEditorCommand because no request was made — the
    single-flight bit is taken by beginEditorCommand BEFORE the snapshot is
    collected, so an early return without release wedges the editor."]
  :boundaries
  ["Editor pane present in the DOM but not displayed (offsetParent null) — the
    ordinary production producer of a null snapshot."
   "Editor pane absent entirely."
   "Snapshot present and normal (the command must remain fully usable)."
   "Save specifically: the header status was already set to 'Saving…' before the
    refusal and must not be left claiming work is in progress."]
  :tests [:editor-hidden-pane-has-no-snapshot
          :editor-save-refuses-missing-snapshot
          :editor-save-back-refuses-missing-snapshot
          :editor-save-file-refuses-missing-snapshot
          :editor-format-refuses-missing-snapshot
          :editor-commands-still-send-with-a-snapshot]
  :rationale "2026-08-21 probe finding 8: saveDraft, saveBackToBook and saveFileDocument all proceeded when collectDraftSync() returned null, posting {sync: null}; only formatDraft refused loudly. A Save pressed while the editor pane was hidden silently committed server state under the reader's gesture."}

 {:id :EDITOR-JDEAD-009
  :status :active
  :ears "While the draft journal is dead, the editor shall remain read-only until a journal snapshot succeeds."
  :misreadings
  ["readOnly is a per-command decoration — it is also the JOURNAL's fail-closed
    state, so a command that blindly restores `readOnly = false` in its finally
    silently re-enables typing that can no longer be recovered after a crash."
   "Restoring readOnly to false IS restoring what I saved — it is only correct
    when the command actually observed false; the restore must read back the
    value it overwrote, not a hardcoded default."
   "A same-identity server edit is exempt because the server already committed
    it — replaceEditorValueAsNativeUndo sets readOnly = false to run
    execCommand, which unlatches the editor as a side effect of a write that is
    itself unjournaled."
   "The latch may be cleared once storage looks healthy again — the only
    evidence that the journal works is a snapshot that actually SUCCEEDED."]
  :boundaries
  ["Journal dies BEFORE a command starts (the command's finally must not clear
    the latch)."
   "Journal dies DURING a command that saved readOnly = false."
   "Healthy journal: the command must still hand back an editable editor."
   "Journal recovers via a later successful snapshot (command assembly or an
    authoritative-paint settle), not via typing — typing is impossible while the
    editor is read-only."]
  :tests [:editor-journal-death-latches-read-only
          :editor-command-cleanup-never-unlatches
          :editor-healthy-command-restores-editable
          :editor-native-undo-refuses-dead-journal
          :editor-successful-snapshot-clears-latch]
  :rationale "2026-08-21 probe findings 5 and 6: the journal's onError set editor.readOnly = true, but formatDraft's finally set readOnly = false unconditionally and replaceEditorValueAsNativeUndo did the same, so the fail-closed state survived only until the next command."}

 {:id :EDITOR-JDEAD-010
  :status :active
  :ears "When journal capture fails while assembling a compound editor command, the client shall refuse to send the command."
  :misreadings
  ["A body missing only its journal fields is still a valid command — it is an
    UNFENCED write: the server's journal-receipt path treats absent metadata as
    a legacy caller and attaches no durable receipt, so the command can never be
    confirmed against the exact visible bytes."
   "The same refusal belongs on the background sync lane — the advisory lane is
    droppable and pre-dates the journal; converting it into a command is a
    different and lossier change, and EDITOR-JDEAD-009 has already made the
    editor read-only so nothing new can accumulate there."
   "An absent journal is the same as a failed journal — strict mode must refuse
    only a FAILED snapshot; a page where the journal was never installed still
    has to be usable."
   "Identity transitions (node open, tab/project switch through
    collectEditorTransitionSync) can share the strict door — a null snapshot
    there DOWNGRADES the frame fence from identity-change to an unfenced
    accepted-operation, so that path deliberately keeps the legacy behavior."]
  :boundaries
  ["Journal installed, snapshot throws (strict → null)."
   "Journal not installed at all (strict → body unchanged)."
   "Journal healthy (strict → body carrying journal fields)."
   "Background sync under the same failure (unchanged legacy behavior: body
    without journal fields, still sent)."]
  :tests [:editor-dead-journal-has-no-command-snapshot
          :editor-command-refuses-unjournaled-snapshot
          :editor-background-sync-keeps-legacy-behavior
          :editor-uninstalled-journal-is-not-dead]
  :rationale "2026-08-21 probe finding 6: augmentEditorSync caught a snapshot failure, notified, and then returned the body WITHOUT journal fields, so Save/Format shipped an unfenced write that could never be durably acknowledged for the exact visible bytes."}

 {:id :EDITOR-CONF-003
  :status :active
  :ears "When a command response arrives with status 409, the client shall raise the conflict surface from that same response before or together with any message that references it."
  :misreadings
  ["A detached promise chain is close enough — postJSON's clone-and-parse ran
    unawaited, so the command's toast (\"choose an action in the red banner\")
    could paint before, or entirely without, the banner it names."
   "Two independent parses of the same 409 are harmless — the second one sees
    the conflict already displayed, the reducer answers 'unchanged', and the
    command concludes nothing was shown. One parse per response, shared."
   "Raising the banner is the command's job — it is the RESPONSE's job; the
    same helper must serve the command lane and the background sync lane so
    there is exactly one parser and one policy."
   "Ordering only matters visually — the toast is the only thing that tells the
    reader where to look, so a toast without its banner is a dead end."]
  :boundaries
  ["Response body that is not JSON at all."
   "JSON body that is not a conflict envelope (no outcome/conflict-id)."
   "The same 409 raised twice (postJSON's detached call and the command's
    awaited call must agree on one outcome)."
   "Save Back and Format, whose 409s take the same door as Save's."]
  :tests [:editor-409-raises-banner-before-toast
          :editor-409-parses-once-per-response
          :editor-save-back-409-raises-conflict]
  :rationale "2026-08-21 probe finding 4: the 409 → banner path was a detached response.clone().json() chain with .catch(() => {}), while saveDraft's 409 branch toasted a reference to a banner whose appearance was neither awaited nor guaranteed."}

 {:id :EDITOR-CONF-004
  :status :active
  :ears "When a 409 conflict payload cannot produce a displayable conflict, the client shall show a loud failure notification stating that the save was refused."
  :misreadings
  ["A silent catch is acceptable because the save simply did not happen — the
    reader keeps typing into text they believe is saved; a refusal nobody hears
    is indistinguishable from data loss."
   "A superseded conflict is an unusable payload — a 409 the reducer ignores as
    belonging to an older generation, or one that repeats a conflict already on
    screen, is correctly handled and must stay SILENT. Loud failure fires only
    when no conflict surface results at all."
   "The generic 'save did not confirm' message covers it — the reader must be
    told the save was REFUSED and that their visible text is intact, not that
    the outcome is unknown."]
  :boundaries
  ["Malformed / non-JSON 409 body."
   "Well-formed body missing conflict-id."
   "Older-generation conflict (reducer effect 'ignore') — silent."
   "Repeat of the conflict already displayed (reducer effect 'unchanged') —
    silent."]
  :tests [:editor-unusable-409-fails-loudly
          :editor-superseded-409-stays-silent]
  :rationale "2026-08-21 probe finding 4: a 409 whose payload failed to parse hit .catch(() => {}) and vanished, leaving a toast pointing at a banner that never appeared — or, on Save Back, no signal at all."}

 {:id :EDITOR-CONF-005
  :status :active
  :ears "When a Format response arrives with status 409, the client shall present it as a save conflict, not as a generic failure."
  :misreadings
  ["Any non-200 from Format means the transform failed — a 409 means the
    OPPOSITE of a failed transform: the server refused because the visible
    snapshot is stale, which is a resolvable conflict with preserved text."
   "'Format was not committed; visible text remains intact' is honest enough —
    it hides the fact that a resolution is available and that autosave is now
    quarantined."
   "Format needs its own conflict handling — it takes the same door as Save so
    the three commands cannot drift apart."]
  :boundaries
  ["Format 409 with a displayable conflict payload."
   "Format 409 whose payload is unusable (falls through to EDITOR-CONF-004)."
   "Format non-200, non-409 (must stay the generic failure message)."]
  :tests [:editor-format-409-is-a-conflict
          :editor-format-non-409-stays-generic]
  :rationale "2026-08-21 probe finding 10: formatDraft tested `response.status !== 200` and reported every refusal as a generic failure, so a Format-time conflict looked like a broken transform."}

 {:id :EDITOR-DURA-007
  :status :active
  :ears "While the draft journal is installed, when a compound editor command response lacks the durable journal acknowledgement for the submitted snapshot, the client shall not report the command as saved."
  :misreadings
  ["The identity/revision headers prove durability — they prove the server
    ACCEPTED a generation, not that these exact bytes were durably written; the
    durable receipt (X-Editor-Durable + sequence/hash/token/document) is the
    only evidence of the second claim."
   "Gate on whether the journal record could be cleared — the local record moves
    on whenever the reader keeps typing during the round trip, so clearing is
    not evidence about the SUBMITTED bytes. Compare the receipt against the
    journal fields the command actually sent."
   "A command that was not confirmed saved may still clear the conflict banner —
    un-saved implies un-resolved; durability is checked first, and only then may
    resolvesConflict / the submitted snapshot be honored."
   "Every command should be gated — a command that carried NO journal fields
    gets no receipt by server design (legacy-legal), so gating it would fail
    every green save. Save File is excluded outright: /api/file/save attaches no
    receipt headers at all (EDITOR-DURA-008, deferred)."]
  :boundaries
  ["Journaled command + valid matching receipt (success stands)."
   "Journaled command + no X-Editor-Durable header (report not saved)."
   "Journaled command + receipt for a DIFFERENT sequence/hash/token."
   "Un-journaled command + header ack only (legacy success preserved)."
   "Save File (explicitly out of scope while DURA-008 is deferred)."]
  :tests [:editor-journaled-save-requires-receipt
          :editor-journaled-save-with-receipt-succeeds
          :editor-unjournaled-save-keeps-legacy-success
          :editor-unconfirmed-save-cannot-clear-conflict



















































































































































